[{:db/id 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b5d70a3f-a448-5de5-8896-cf994f65054e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133719 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ \\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\,R\\phantom{|}}\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $R$ and $Q^T.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b5d70a3f-a448-5de5-8896-cf994f65054e" :question/id #uuid "b5d70a3f-a448-5de5-8896-cf994f65054e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139751" :question/problem "$$\nx={R}^{-1}\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & ={R}^{-1}\\,\\,\\bbox[6px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{|}{Q}^{T}\\,\\,}\\,\\,\\bbox[6px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{|}b\\phantom{|}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${Q}^{T}$ and $b.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75513403-98f8-5580-8da1-119fb356a265"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75513403-98f8-5580-8da1-119fb356a265" :answer-field/id #uuid "75513403-98f8-5580-8da1-119fb356a265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eef0bb49-3a2a-51f2-9a63-905441460146" :answer-field/choices ["answer-29c6894b-ca73-55e4-b9e5-4515a24ac505" "answer-efe2b6b7-9514-56b9-905d-32ae3b168ebe" "answer-4dd231d7-89bb-5f54-b089-0db260de5eac" "answer-2b99f2e8-4b05-5e94-b564-250f2853615a" "answer-eef0bb49-3a2a-51f2-9a63-905441460146"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29c6894b-ca73-55e4-b9e5-4515a24ac505" :answer/id #uuid "29c6894b-ca73-55e4-b9e5-4515a24ac505" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $b$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efe2b6b7-9514-56b9-905d-32ae3b168ebe" :answer/id #uuid "efe2b6b7-9514-56b9-905d-32ae3b168ebe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ and $b$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dd231d7-89bb-5f54-b089-0db260de5eac" :answer/id #uuid "4dd231d7-89bb-5f54-b089-0db260de5eac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$b$ and $Q$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b99f2e8-4b05-5e94-b564-250f2853615a" :answer/id #uuid "2b99f2e8-4b05-5e94-b564-250f2853615a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$m\\cdot {Q}^{-1}$ and $b$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eef0bb49-3a2a-51f2-9a63-905441460146" :answer/id #uuid "eef0bb49-3a2a-51f2-9a63-905441460146" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${Q}^{T}$ and $b$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186401097 :question/problem "$$\nx=\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the expression above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nThen, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by ${R}^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{-1}\\cdot Rx & ={R}^{-1}\\cdot {Q}^{T}b \\\\ x & =\\bbox[6px,border: 1px solid black]{\\,\\phantom{|}\\,\\,{R}^{-1}\\,}\\,{Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are ${R}^{-1}$ and ${Q}^{T}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186363391 :question/problem "$$\nR\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}=\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{|}x\\phantom{|}} & ={Q}^{T}\\,b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing parts of the equation are $x$ and ${Q}^{T}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
